  <section id="s1">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>1. Dynamical Descent, Output-Null Fibers, and Non-Ergodic Population Averaging</h2>
      <span class="mod-tag">neuro/descent.cub · lib/observation.cub · lib/fiber.cub</span>
    </div>
    <p>Every neural recording — whether single-unit spike trains, Utah arrays, calcium imaging, or EEG — is a lossy projection \(o : H \to Y\) from high-dimensional biophysical state (ion-channel gating variables, dendritic voltages, dynamic synaptic resources, glial potassium gradients) onto a low-dimensional readout \(y\).</p>
    <p>In <code>neuro/descent.cub</code>, this partial observation is formalized directly through the observation fiber \(\mathsf{Fib}_o(y) = \sum_{h : H} \mathsf{Path}_Y(o(h), y)\). Three foundational obstructions follow immediately:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Non-closure of voltage-only projections (Hodgkin–Huxley obstruction):</strong> Two membrane states with identical voltage \(V\) and identical input current \(I\), but distinct sodium-channel inactivation variables \(h_1 \neq h_2\), lie in the same voltage fiber yet produce strictly different voltage derivatives \(\dot{V}_1 \neq \dot{V}_2\). No autonomous dynamical law descends to voltage alone.</li>
      <li><strong>Output-null fibers &amp; intrinsic manifolds:</strong> For a linear or nonlinear BCI decoder \(D : \mathbb{R}^N \to \mathbb{R}^k\), the fiber \(\mathsf{Fib}_D(y)\) is the output-null subspace of neural states producing the same cursor or prosthetic command \(y\). Replacing a decoder \(D\) with \(D'\) that preserves the <em>present</em> output \(D'(x) = D(x)\) still alters the task gradient and Hessian with respect to neural activity — explaining why outside-manifold perturbations disrupt biological learning even when instantaneous output matches.</li>
      <li><strong>Non-ergodic population averaging:</strong> Averaging trajectories across distinct individuals or heterogeneous cortical populations collapses incompatible fibers into a synthetic mean trajectory that need not exist in any individual nervous system.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="s2">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>2. Coupled Two-Learner Co-Adaptation: Symbolic SOS Lyapunov Stability Over \(\mathbb{Q}\)</h2>
      <span class="mod-tag">neuro/lyapunov.cub · chem/algebra.cub</span>
    </div>
    <p>In a closed-loop brain–computer interface, <em>both</em> the biological nervous system \(B\) and the adaptive decoder \(C\) learn simultaneously. Let \(e_B, e_C\) be their coupled tracking errors evolving under self-adaptation rates \(\eta_B, \eta_C &gt; 0\) and cross-coupling gains \(\gamma_1, \gamma_2\):</p>
    $$\dot{e}_B \;=\; -\eta_B e_B + \gamma_1 e_C, \qquad \dot{e}_C \;=\; \gamma_2 e_B - \eta_C e_C$$
    <p>Consider the general weighted quadratic Lyapunov functional \(V(e_B, e_C) = p_1 e_B^2 + p_2 e_C^2\) with weights \(p_1, p_2 &gt; 0\). Its time derivative along trajectories is:</p>
    $$-\dot{V} \;=\; 2p_1\eta_B e_B^2 \;-\; 2(p_1\gamma_1 + p_2\gamma_2)e_B e_C \;+\; 2p_2\eta_C e_C^2$$
    <p>Rather than checking numerical eigenvalues at isolated sample points, <code>neuro/lyapunov.cub</code> proves the exact <strong>Sum-of-Squares (SOS) completion-of-the-square identity</strong> in canonical normal form over the universal polynomial ring over \(\mathbb{Q}\) (<code>al-id</code> in <code>chem/algebra.cub</code>):</p>
    $$2p_1\eta_B\,(-\dot{V}) \;=\; \bigl(2p_1\eta_B e_B - (p_1\gamma_1 + p_2\gamma_2)e_C\bigr)^2 \;+\; \Bigl(4p_1 p_2 \eta_B \eta_C - (p_1\gamma_1 + p_2\gamma_2)^2\Bigr)e_C^2$$
    <div class="thm">
      <b>Checked Universal Certificate — Exact Hurwitz Co-Adaptation Threshold (neuro/lyapunov.cub)</b>
      Because the identity is proved in the universal polynomial ring over \(\mathbb{Q}\), it holds in every commutative ring containing \(\mathbb{Q}\) for <em>all</em> parameter values. Whenever \(4p_1 p_2 \eta_B \eta_C &gt; (p_1\gamma_1 + p_2\gamma_2)^2\), every term on the right is strictly nonnegative, proving global asymptotic stability. Optimizing the weight ratio \(p_1/p_2 = \gamma_2/\gamma_1\) recovers the sharp Hurwitz boundary:
      $$\eta_B\,\eta_C \;>\; \gamma_1\,\gamma_2$$
      If cross-coupling exceeds the geometric mean of brain and decoder learning rates, co-adaptation destabilizes; below it, joint convergence is algebraically guaranteed.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="s3">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>3. Forward-Invariant Safety: Control Barrier Functions &amp; Driven FitzHugh–Nagumo</h2>
      <span class="mod-tag">neuro/barrier.cub · chem/algebra.cub</span>
    </div>
    <p>Asymptotic stability alone does not prevent a closed-loop neural interface from temporarily overshooting into an unsafe stimulation regime. Safety requires <strong>forward invariance</strong> of a safe set \(h(e_B, e_C) = R^2 - e_B^2 - e_C^2 \ge 0\) via Nagumo's Control Barrier Function (CBF) condition \(\dot{h} + \alpha h \ge 0\).</p>

    <h3>3.1 Coupled 2-Learner CBF Safety Certificate (<code>cbf-2learner-safe-sos-id</code>)</h3>
    <p>In <code>neuro/barrier.cub</code>, the CBF margin \(\dot{h} + \alpha h\) is proved equal in \(\mathbb{Q}[e_B, e_C, R, \eta_B, \eta_C, \gamma, \alpha]\) to an exact Sum-of-Squares decomposition yielding the closed-form safety criterion:</p>
    $$2\eta_B \;>\; \alpha \;>\; 0 \qquad \text{and} \qquad (2\eta_B - \alpha)(2\eta_C - \alpha) \;\ge\; 4\gamma^2$$
    <p>Whenever those two inequalities hold, any trajectory starting inside the safe ball \(e_B^2 + e_C^2 \le R^2\) is mathematically guaranteed never to leave it.</p>

    <h3>3.2 Globally Driven FitzHugh–Nagumo Excitable Membrane (<code>fhn-driven-trapping-sos-id</code>)</h3>
    <p>For single-neuron and excitable-tissue dynamics, consider the full externally driven FitzHugh–Nagumo system:</p>
    $$\dot{v} \;=\; v - \tfrac{1}{3}v^3 - w + I, \qquad \dot{w} \;=\; \varepsilon(v + a - bw)$$
    <p><code>neuro/barrier.cub</code> proves a universal polynomial SOS trapping-region certificate holding for <strong>every</strong> stimulation current \(I\), recovery offset \(a\), and positive damping \(b &gt; 0\) — establishing boundedness of membrane potential under arbitrary bounded neural stimulation without numerical integration.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="s4">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>4. Proof-Gated Neural Interfaces, Reshaping Boundaries, and Consent</h2>
      <span class="mod-tag">lib/polyproc.cub · neuro/coproc.cub · neuro/reshaping-coproc.cub</span>
    </div>
    <p>Static process signatures cannot model a nervous system or BCI whose active interface changes mid-run — opening or closing ion channels, coupling or decoupling a neural decoder, or binding a new sensory modality. In <code>lib/polyproc.cub</code>, Hyperactivity generalizes static guarded processes to <strong>indexed interface-reshaping processes</strong> <code>RProc κ D A i</code> over a dynamic signature <code>DynSig</code>, where each command–response pair \((c, r)\) moves the continuation to a new interface state <code>ds-next D i c r</code> (with an exact cubical round-trip embedding path <code>gproc-rproc-id</code> back to static processes).</p>

    <h3>4.1 Proof-Gated Activation (<code>SafeGateDS</code> &amp; <code>disagreement-blocks-gate</code>)</h3>
    <p><code>neuro/reshaping-coproc.cub</code> instantiates <code>DynSig</code> to construct a <strong>proof-gated neural co-processor</strong> with two interface modes: passive probing (<code>false</code>) and active coupled actuation (<code>true</code>). The transition command from <code>false</code> to <code>true</code> requires an explicit <code>SafeAt</code> witness over the entire compatible observation fiber:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Unverified activation is ill-typed:</strong> The runtime does not "check a boolean flag" — without a proof over \(\mathsf{Fib}_o(y)\), the activation command cannot even be constructed.</li>
      <li><strong>Disagreement blocks activation (<code>disagreement-blocks-gate</code>):</strong> Any split witness (<code>Disagreement</code> from <code>chem/basin-compose.cub</code>) refutes warrant and makes activation uninhabited until an active-sensing query narrows the fiber.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4.2 Lexical-Gate Impossibility &amp; Epistemic Asymmetry (<code>neuro/coproc.cub</code>)</h3>
    <p>Recent inner-speech decoding proposals (e.g., Kunz et al., <em>Cell</em> 2025) suggest protecting neural privacy using a "mental password" (a lexical trigger word). <code>neuro/coproc.cub</code> proves structurally why a lexical gate cannot constitute genuine consent: surface lexical tokens share fibers with distinct internal intents and involuntary thoughts. Conversely, transparent receptive bidirectional lenses contract the observer's fiber to a singleton (<code>isContr</code>), whereas covert hidden-objective stimulus optimizers (such as engagement-maximization feeds) leave the objective fiber completely unconstrained.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="s5">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>5. Cognitive Inhibition, REBUS Prior Relaxation, and Framing Invariance</h2>
      <span class="mod-tag">neuro/inhibition.cub · chem/basin-compose.cub · docs/exposition-language-inhibition.cub</span>
    </div>
    <p>In <code>neuro/inhibition.cub</code>, cognitive control and perceptual dynamics are formalized over the exact same observation-fiber and compositional-basin primitives:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Prior relaxation (REBUS / reduced latent inhibition):</strong> Loosening rigid top-down priors corresponds to relaxing fiber restrictions so previously pruned states re-enter the active hypothesis space, enabling escape from trapped local attractor basins.</li>
      <li><strong>Framing-effect invariance (De Martino et al.):</strong> Two descriptions of the same underlying decision problem related by a value-preserving equivalence must induce identical warranted choices — exposing emotional framing bias as a failure of univalent transport across equivalent presentations.</li>
      <li><strong>Addiction &amp; engagement attractor collapse:</strong> Modeling compulsive engagement loops as deep, narrow attractor basins that shrink an organism's reachable state space unless broken by structural basin reshaping (<code>MetaFold</code>).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="s6">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>6. Synthetic Synesthesia as Cross-Modal Interface Reshaping</h2>
      <span class="mod-tag">neuro/synesthesia.cub · neuro/reshaping-coproc.cub · docs/language-is-trained-synesthesia.md</span>
    </div>
    <p>Language itself is trained cross-modal synesthesia: arbitrary acoustic or visual tokens become bound to structured semantic invariants through repeated co-occurrence. <code>neuro/synesthesia.cub</code> formalizes spoken language as a non-sonic concurrent, bidirectional literacy/tickertape transport, memory transport \(M_i(w, t)\), and <strong>population-deviation detector blindness</strong> — proving why a detector calibrated solely on statistical deviation from a population norm becomes blind to a phenomenon once prevalence approaches 100% (as with universal literacy).</p>
    <p>In <code>neuro/reshaping-coproc.cub</code>, deliberate training of a new cross-modal binding (such as grapheme–color or syntax–geometry synesthesia) is instantiated directly via <code>SynesthesiaDS</code> and <code>install-synesthesia</code> as a typed interface-reshaping transition that permanently expands the active sensory signature of the nervous system.</p>
  </section>

